WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.590
- I want to react to one
specific thing you said

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:04.540
about the angry men.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.173
It is 100% projection.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.130
The angry young men and
women are on the left.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.850
How could they not be?

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.320
You're living in a racist,
sexist, misogynist,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.400
homophobic, xenophobic society.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.560
You are a victim.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.340
Unless you are a white, heterosexual,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.650
Christian male, you are a victim.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.790
Who is producing anger, us?

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.080
Saying, you know what?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.950
Life is inherently difficult.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.490
Now what do you do?

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.720
Make your bed.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.056
Start with that.

00:00:36.056 --> 00:00:38.473
(calm music)

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.620
Hi everybody, I'm Dennis Prager,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.850
and Dave says I don't need an introduction

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.090
which is a very major compliment.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:55.300
I'm not sure it's true, but,
well, it doesn't matter.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.133
You're here.

00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:57.970
You're here because of Dave Rubin anyway.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:00:59.507
So we're gonna talk about

00:00:59.507 --> 00:01:02.060
"Don't Burn This Book" chapter nine.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.800
I am very delighted that David asked me

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.760
to comment on chapter nine.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.880
It's find a mentor.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.664
It's largely about Jordan Peterson,

00:01:11.664 --> 00:01:12.710
but of course it's even larger than,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.670
by the way, let me interrupt myself.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.070
I think that Jordan Peterson,
I wrote the introduction

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.520
to his biography, I think that he is one

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.770
of the most wonderful human
beings that I have met

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.650
in my life and I cannot tell you

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.020
how many wonderful people I have met.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.020
This is an extraordinary
human being in every way,

00:01:36.944 --> 00:01:41.480
and like many extraordinary
people, he bears the weight

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.893
of the world on his shoulders
because he's so sensitive

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.360
to the pain of the world
and he wants to try

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.190
and make a dent in this
tremendous amount of pain.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.477
I have often said on my radio show that

00:02:00.477 --> 00:02:04.510
if pain were water, the world would drown.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:09.510
So I resonate to him
tremendously and you, Dave,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.180
obviously do and what your
chapter, while it's mostly

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.860
about Jordan, is about the
subject of finding a mentor.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.950
So let me tell you something you'll find,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.940
I think, of interest.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.830
I don't know, I've been
broadcasting 35 years,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.830
so I started in my 30s, and
so I was a kid when I started

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.540
and I'm not a kid now.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.520
So gradually over the course of time,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.090
more and more people would
say, calling into the show,

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.183
Dennis, I just want you to know
you're like a father to me,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.810
and I've always answered,
good, everyone needs one,

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.260
and I've said all the time
it is every male's task

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.023
to become a father figure to younger men.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:10.730
That is our task, and that
is why every human being,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.860
and I'll talk about men now,
every man can make a difference

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.210
whether you have children or not.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.380
Whatever your circumstance,
if you are a father figure

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.980
to one younger man, the younger
man may be 10 years younger

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.980
than you, you have made
a dent in this world,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.550
but a lot of men, like a lot of women,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.410
don't want to grow up.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.260
So the last thing they want

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.443
to be is a father figure to anybody.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.220
They want to be the
peer of younger people,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.440
not the father figure of younger people.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.670
So I can't tell you how much
I resonate to that chapter,

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:54.670
even the part about dressing
formally just as you are now.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.800
I mean, it's dramatic.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.070
- This is my favorite black
shirt, come on, Prager.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:01.920
- I'm sure it is.

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:06.340
- You are definitely dressing
appropriately for the chapter.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.980
You know, a few things that
you said there really strike me

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.450
as interesting because, you
know, one of the main things

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.910
that we heard throughout the tour

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:16.420
as the hit pieces would
come out in the media is

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.420
that the only people who show
up to these Jordan Peterson

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.990
events are angry young
men, and it was interesting

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.190
because not only was
that factually not true,

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.140
it was usually about
60, 40 male to female,

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.630
but they weren't angry
as far as I could tell.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.300
It was a lot of people that
were trying to fix their lives

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.950
and they were, of course, every age,

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.930
and there were literally
grandmother groups that showed up

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.740
and their were trans
people there and everything

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:42.920
that there could possibly
be, but this idea

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:46.050
that he was only talking to
angry young men I thought

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.560
was sort of fascinating
because if it was true,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.150
let's just pretend for a second that

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.950
what they were saying was
true, well, if you're talking

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.670
to angry young men and you're
making their lives better,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.800
helping them get away from anger, well,

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.280
would that not be considered good?

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.250
But I know the media is sort of unable

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.520
to deal with somewhat complex topics.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.850
So to see that and then
to see that, you're right,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:13.150
there has been some weird
shift that I think you have

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.930
because of radio and
because of being public

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.830
about your opinions for so
long, you've definitely seen

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:23.010
where something happened that
is not just specific to men,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.982
but perhaps has more to do with young men

00:05:25.982 --> 00:05:30.982
where the father figure or
the trusted uncle or someone

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.500
that would give them guidance on really

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.130
what the hard things are in the world,

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.640
to really be responsible about your life,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.110
and just because you don't like your job,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.809
it doesn't mean you can quit it tomorrow

00:05:41.809 --> 00:05:44.220
if you've got family to feed,

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.940
and just really, what does he say?

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:46.820
I mean, simple stuff.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.250
Clean your room before
you clean the world.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.860
Stand up straight with
your shoulders back.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.300
Like these messages, he
would say it sometimes

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.133
during the tour.

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.090
He would say, some of my
messages are not rocket science,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.980
but they're long-forgotten
truths, and I think you're a guy

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.010
that's been trying to fight
for long-forgotten truths

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:02.853
for a long time.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.048
- Yes, I mean look, there's
so many things you said

00:06:07.048 --> 00:06:09.613
that I would love to react to.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.090
We are on this world, on this planet

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.840
for a limited period of
time, and you have to decide

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.930
what you want to do with your life.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:27.670
I want to touch people
to lead a better life.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:32.113
In this terms, better
meaning deeper and more good,

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.810
but I want to react to one
specific thing you said

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.780
about the angry men.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.947
It is 100% projection.

00:06:40.947 --> 00:06:45.360
The angry young men and
women are on the left.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.070
How could they not be?

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.550
You're living in a racist,
sexist, misogynist,

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:52.630
homophobic, xenophobic society.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.800
You are a victim.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.560
Unless you are a white, heterosexual,

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.880
Christian male, you are a victim.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.020
Who is producing anger, us?

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.310
Saying, you know what?

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:05.190
Life is inherently difficult.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.730
Now what do you do?

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.629
Make your bed, start with that.

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:13.780
We are, I wrote a book on happiness.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.720
That's not exactly an anger-inducing book.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.363
I've had Happiness Hour
on my show for 21 years.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.810
They create anger.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.110
We're trying to subdue it and make happy,

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.413
better, richer people.

00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:29.933
Now, I don't mean richer
in money, richer in life.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:32.460
- I wonder, Dennis, do you think

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.820
that everyone actually needs a mentor?

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.560
Because one of the
things that I talk about

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.090
in the chapter is that it's
not that Jordan and I sat down,

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.510
it's not that he sat me
down and said, hey Dave,

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.170
we're going on tour and I'm
gonna impart some wisdom on you.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.450
It wasn't some sort of Jedi
sit down kind of thing.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.930
It really was that through the year

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.230
and a half we spent together,
I saw him doing the best

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:58.520
that he could and I saw
him sometimes admitting

00:07:58.520 --> 00:07:59.870
what his shortcomings were.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:02.707
I saw him sometimes in
front of 5,000 people.

00:08:02.707 --> 00:08:06.560
If a pointy, you know, a
specifically pointed question

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.000
was asked about something,
if people asked, for example,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.760
about his carnivore diet and
they would ask for advice

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.950
about their diet, he would
say, well, I'm not a dietician.

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:15.990
I'm not a nutritionist.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.110
I can tell you what works for me,

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:21.070
and I think hearing
somebody with some humility

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:24.800
and more than anything else
try to put truth in the world,

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.050
I think I got that by osmosis,

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.260
but it wasn't really a sit down.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:32.740
I almost didn't realize that
I needed it until I got it.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:34.270
Does that make sense?

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:38.840
- Totally, totally, but your
opening question is a variant.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.150
Does everyone need a mentor?

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:46.150
The answer is yes, absolutely,
but the mentor can come

00:08:46.322 --> 00:08:50.000
in different shapes, forms, and ways.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.753
So I believe my father
was not a loving father,

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.743
but he was a mentor which
frankly is more important.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:05.750
I will give up 1,000 hugs
for 1,000 sources of wisdom.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:10.120
No, for 10 wisdoms, okay?

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.520
I would like both, but
if I have to choose,

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.590
there's no question in my mind.

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:19.100
So in my early life, and
I had him as a model.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.170
That I did, he was.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.170
He was a model of a man
and boys need male models.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.560
Number two, I had mentors
from 3,500 years ago.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.520
I'm a religious Jew.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.100
I've written a bible commentary,
"The Rational Bible,"

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:44.100
and the figures there, Moses was a mentor.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.890
I know that to many of
your secular viewers,

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:52.000
this sounds almost maybe silly even

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:57.000
or certainly odd, but it's true.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:02.020
I studied this man all of
my life and he has flaws.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:08.540
That's the beauty of the
Old Testament figures.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.100
They're flawed.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:14.570
So I can't relate to a perfect
person, but I can relate

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:17.083
to a flawed man who does great things.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:23.550
So historical figures,
a Churchill, mentors,

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.170
I'll give you one more example.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.540
My dearest friend, doctor Stephen
Marmer, is a psychiatrist.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:33.000
He tells the story of a
patient from many years ago.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.050
His father was a traveling salesman.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.090
Came home only on weekends.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:40.660
So he'd spend the whole
time with his wife.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.720
He wanted to reconnect with
his wife on the weekend.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.980
He didn't have time for his kids.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:46.480
So what did the son do?

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.100
He went to the movies.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:53.100
In those days, the figures
in movies were male mentors.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.130
He got to be a man from the movies.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:00.020
That's not available today.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.863
The men in movies are largely children.

00:11:04.736 --> 00:11:07.700
- There's so much there because it's like,

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.650
I think people are seeing that
they're missing something,

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.890
and in many ways it's why PragerU works,

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:16.210
it's why Shapiro's message gets across.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:19.470
It's that something got lost in the last,

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.720
I suspect you'd think it's
longer than I would think,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.840
but I would say in the
last two decades maybe,

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.990
but particularly in the last decade

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:28.940
where everybody just thought, whatever,

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.020
life is just whatever you
want at any given moment

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.660
and if you grab it, you grab it, great,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:36.280
and you just keep going, where
now there is some other thing

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:37.400
that's working for people.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:40.180
So I suspect that when
you read the chapter,

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:44.120
when I talk about finding the
importance of biblical stories

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.210
and as you know, we're
expanding our family here,

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:50.850
and those are messages that I
kept getting through Jordan.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:52.970
You know, he said something
that's a little counter

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:54.850
to what you said before,
but not that far off.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:58.517
I would hear him say something
like, for almost everybody,

00:11:59.957 --> 00:12:02.546
being a parent is a crucial part of life.

00:12:02.546 --> 00:12:05.570
Not for everybody, but
for almost everybody,

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.240
and what I kept thinking around
that, you know my husband,

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.220
David, you and Sue have
been here for dinner.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:14.010
I knew that David wanted kids
and I didn't particularly

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.286
want them because I had
put so much investment--

00:12:16.286 --> 00:12:19.773
- So you married up, man, you married up.

00:12:19.773 --> 00:12:22.360
- But I did marry up, that is for sure,

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.280
and you've had the chicken.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.409
You said it was the best
chicken you ever had,

00:12:26.409 --> 00:12:31.210
but I thought as I
heard Jordan saying that

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.690
that I started thinking, well,

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.540
could I be the exception to the rule?

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.280
Could I be the exception of the person

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.790
that could not have kids
and feel fully realized?

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.090
And then on top of that
fact, well then, what would

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:44.050
that make me to the person
I married if I'm not going

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:47.850
to allow that person to feel
fully fulfilled in their life?

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.760
So he moved me.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:51.930
In many ways, it was almost
accidental because again,

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:53.940
he wasn't sending these messages to me,

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:56.483
but they were messages through osmosis.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:04.540
- The problem with what you're
saying, oh, and I 100% agree

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.680
with it, but the problem
with what you're saying

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:11.680
to vast numbers of young
people now is the idea

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:18.190
of growing up is just not a value.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:19.950
It's not thought of.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:23.303
When I was a kid, again, in
a religious Jewish setting,

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:29.750
all I was told is be a
mench which is a man,

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:31.860
and I mean, what did a man mean?

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.140
A man means you grow up and
you take responsibility.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:36.200
You'll find this interesting.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.760
On my male female hour,
I ask women, just women,

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:44.760
call in for the next hour, what
do you define as masculine?

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:49.930
And do you know, I did not
know what they would say.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:54.090
Overwhelmingly, they said
the sexiest man is the man

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:55.593
who takes responsibility.

00:13:56.906 --> 00:13:58.520
- It doesn't surprise me.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:13:59.909
It really doesn't surprise
- No, I'm sure it doesn't.

00:13:59.909 --> 00:14:00.742
- me actually.

00:14:00.742 --> 00:14:02.333
- Yes.

00:14:02.333 --> 00:14:06.490
So I knew, I knew from the earliest age,

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.605
I have to get married, I have
to have children, have to.

00:14:09.605 --> 00:14:12.300
It's not a matter of, gee, do I want to?

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.453
No, no, Dennis, you want to
grow up, this is what you do.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.390
- So one of the things
that we've talked about

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.390
and I've had you on the show
obviously a couple times

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:22.420
and I've once had you in
here to debate morality

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.240
with Michael Shermer who comes

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.050
from a nonbeliever perspective,
but in the few pages

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.990
that I was writing about
the biblical stories

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:30.840
and how I have come to believe

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.670
that we need these eternal
truths to organize a society,

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:37.050
I suspect you were probably,
you know, you must have had

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:40.820
a little smile on your face
reading that, but do you agree

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.810
with my premise that on the micro-level

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.360
that individually you
could be a nonbeliever

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:49.910
and be purely moral and good
and all of those things?

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:53.230
Because my argument is not that
you can't do it personally.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.800
I think it's very hard and
I think most people can't.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.720
I had John Kasich in here once
and he said something sort

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:00.070
of off the cuff that I ended up thinking

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:02.880
was really quite profound.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.720
He said, you know, a lot of
people could do it without God,

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.400
I'm just not one of those people.

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:09.830
I thought that was actually
a pretty moving statement,

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.700
and I think I've come around
to a belief that's very similar

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:16.480
to that, but I do believe that
individually people can do it

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.760
without God or without a set
of rules outside of themselves,

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:23.150
but that a society simply
cannot organize itself

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:23.983
without it and--

00:15:23.983 --> 00:15:24.924
- That's exactly it.

00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:29.924
So it's like society needs
people to wear seat belts.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:33.070
Does every individual
need to wear a seat belt?

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:36.510
No, but society needs to wear a seat belt.

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:40.030
Religion is a seat belt, and by the way,

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:41.390
of course there were atheists

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:43.429
and there were good secular people.

00:15:43.429 --> 00:15:46.300
It's not debatable, but
there were good people

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:47.533
who believed in Zeus.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:52.420
There were good people
who worshiped 406 Gods.

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:53.780
So therefore, what?

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.560
It doesn't prove anything.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:59.003
Yes, the conscience is
universal, but it's not enough.

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:05.019
I give the example, you
want me to say it or

00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:06.060
do you just want
- Yeah go ahead.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:06.893
- To say something?

00:16:06.893 --> 00:16:10.772
Okay, so I opened up my
book "The Rational Bible."

00:16:10.772 --> 00:16:14.930
It's now two of five
volumes, Genesis and Exodus,

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.630
and I tell the story.

00:16:16.630 --> 00:16:19.920
I had a difficult time with
my parents in my late teens

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.000
and my early 20s which is hardly shocking.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Okay, but I treated them with
respect every day of my life,

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:32.890
no matter how difficult
my relationship was,

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:36.100
and there was only one reason, only one.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:41.100
I believe that God had
instructed me honor your father

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.643
and your mother, period.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.680
Afterwards, I did it because I loved them,

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:51.120
but when I didn't love
them, I still did it

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.433
because there was a God
who told me to do so.

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:59.680
- Yeah, I mean I think that
really is the undergirding point

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:00.570
that I'm talking about.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:04.230
The thing outside of us
that can help organize us.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.930
So one other thing on
Jordan before we finish up.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.650
I obviously in the book, I
finished writing the book

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.040
in July, we're now in May.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.400
So you know, it takes quite some time

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:14.415
to get these things actually out there,

00:17:14.415 --> 00:17:17.750
and it was a little unclear
exactly what was going on

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:19.850
with Jordan when I was writing it.

00:17:19.850 --> 00:17:23.040
I have seen him since and
as I keep telling everybody,

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.590
I can't say anything
that's not fully public,

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.380
but he is getting better and all that,

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:32.040
but I'm wondering did you ever
see or have callers told you

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.320
about moments when someone
that they really believed

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:38.670
in suddenly had a tumble like that?

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:40.610
And you know, and I do
want to add one other thing

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:44.620
about this that he was
very upfront and would talk

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:47.308
about it often that he
was taking a small amount

00:17:47.308 --> 00:17:50.580
of medication for anti-anxiety,
and then as you know,

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:52.770
his wife was diagnosed
with terminal cancer.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:54.490
They thought that was the end for her.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:55.890
I was literally at lunch with him

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.030
when he got the call about that.

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:01.030
On top of the fame, the
hatred, the hit pieces,

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:03.730
the love, the travel, I
mean, just everything,

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.040
and of course this is for a guy

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.430
that was a clinical psychologist,

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.190
not somebody who thought he was gonna be

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:10.750
the leading intellectual in
the world, but I'm curious

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:12.850
if you have any experience
in that sort of thing?

00:18:12.850 --> 00:18:15.710
When people look up to
somebody and it's like none

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:20.130
of us are, if I can say
Jesus to a devout Jew,

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.500
it's like a certain amount
of people probably thought

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.720
he was Jesus, like some other
thing is really what I mean,

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.763
like almost not human, and
yet none of us are that.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:33.210
- I have discussed this
on my show all of my life.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:36.801
I've actually read on the
air letters like this.

00:18:36.801 --> 00:18:37.634
Dear Dennis--

00:18:37.634 --> 00:18:38.863
- We've got a minute,
Dennis, bring us home here.

00:18:38.863 --> 00:18:41.200
- Okay, yep, I'll get it done in a minute.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:44.740
Dear Dennis, I want you
to know I have admired you

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:49.410
for 25 years and I heard
you say that X, Y, or Z,

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.550
and I realized you're a
fraud, and I would write back

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.793
and say, so how did I
fool you for 25 years?

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:01.500
This is a danger that people have.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:02.870
There is no perfect being,

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.530
and by the way, why is he less perfect

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:07.833
because he takes an anti-anxiety pill?

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:11.343
It's such a bizarre thing.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:13.960
It's not even a flaw.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:18.062
It's not like, you know, he
was caught having an affair,

00:19:18.062 --> 00:19:21.460
and even then I would still
say, okay, humans are weak.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.650
- I would still think he's one
of the greatest people I knew

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:26.000
but this isn't even a flaw.

00:19:27.883 --> 00:19:29.910
He's taking care of himself.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.183
He deserves to take care of himself.

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:36.153
You'll never admire anyone
if they have to be perfect.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:39.750
- Yeah, that's why they
say don't meet your heroes.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.510
I think that's usually what
they're trying to tell you.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:45.700
Well, with that in mind, even
though I wrote Jordan Peterson

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.900
as the mentor, I do include
you in that group of people

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:52.050
who have helped me clean up
my life and clean up sort

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.320
of everything that I'm doing
here, and I look forward

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.380
to having you and Sue over
for dinner again soon,

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:00.210
and then when we post a picture
about it, people will say

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.730
that you're a homophobe
and I'm a self-hating gay

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:07.200
and you know, the usual, the usual stuff,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:08.163
but we know the truth
- But that's proof.

00:20:08.163 --> 00:20:11.750
That's proof, but get a bigger menorah.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:12.900
- I know, you want a bigger, all right,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:13.940
I've gotta work on the menorah.

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:15.140
I'm sure you know a guy.

